<h2>The background</h2>
<p>The parties married in Andhra Pradesh in April 2003 under Hindu rites. The husband settled in the United Kingdom; his attempts at divorce and custody in Indian courts failed, and he withdrew his appeal. He became a British citizen in February 2013, then obtained a decree from the Cambridge County Court — nisi in November 2013, absolute in January 2014 — on the ground of irretrievable breakdown, his wife having objected to that court&rsquo;s jurisdiction. Weeks later he remarried in England. The first wife lodged a complaint in 2016; the chargesheet named the husband and his parents, with the second wife added at cognizance. The Andhra Pradesh High Court refused to quash, and the matter travelled to the Supreme Court through special leave.</p>

<h2>Recognition: the Narasimha Rao discipline</h2>
<p>The Court&rsquo;s matrimonial analysis follows the classic private-international-law framework of <em>Y. Narasimha Rao</em>, <em>Satya v. Teja Singh</em> and <em>Kishorekumar Mohan Kale</em>. A foreign decree must satisfy Section 13 CPC; for parties married under the Hindu Marriage Act, the foreign court&rsquo;s decree must ordinarily rest on a ground the HMA recognises, and the respondent must have submitted to that forum. Irretrievable breakdown is not an HMA ground, and the wife&rsquo;s appearance under jurisdictional protest did not amount to submission.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment:</strong> &ldquo;Such a decree cannot automatically be accorded recognition in India.&rdquo; And on the citizenship argument: &ldquo;legal consequences flowing from the subsisting marriage cannot be defeated by a subsequent change in the nationality&rdquo;.</p>
</div>
<p>With the first marriage subsisting in Indian law, the second marriage grounded a prima facie Section 494 case. The territorial objection — that the second marriage took place abroad — failed because the subsisting marriage was solemnised in India, and the Court treated bigamy as a continuing offence in answering the delay plea.</p>

<h2>Individual liability: the co-accused go free</h2>
<p>The appeals of the parents and the second wife succeeded. Conspiracy and abetment need an agreement or overt act, and the record showed none against the parents; the second wife could not be prosecuted absent prima facie material that she knew of the subsisting marriage. The result tracks the Court&rsquo;s consistent reluctance, visible in <em>S. Nitheen</em> and related authority, to let Section 494 complaints sweep in the groom&rsquo;s family wholesale.</p>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For NRI spouses.</strong> A foreign divorce obtained on grounds alien to Indian matrimonial law, against a non-submitting spouse, settles nothing in India — and a remarriage on its strength risks prosecution. The safe sequence is an Indian decree, or a foreign decree within the Section 13 window (including genuine submission by both parties).</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For the spouse left in India.</strong> Objecting to the foreign court&rsquo;s jurisdiction, in writing and at the first opportunity, preserved everything in this case. Silence or participation on merits could have been read as submission.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For second spouses and families.</strong> The judgment is equally significant for whom it releases: criminal exposure under Section 494 requires individual, prima facie complicity — knowledge for the second spouse, agreement or overt acts for relatives.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>Three decades after <em>Y. Narasimha Rao</em>, the transnational Hindu marriage remains governed by the same rule: the law under which a marriage is solemnised controls its dissolution, and forum-shopping through emigration and naturalisation does not loosen that grip. As cross-border matrimonial litigation grows through Delhi&rsquo;s courts — recognition suits, maintenance claims, Section 494 complaints — this judgment supplies a current, authoritative restatement, along with a measured limit on the criminal process&rsquo;s reach into the wider family.</p>
